1
00:00:00,380 --> 00:00:04,085
Focusing sunlight and the solar cooker. How a concave

2
00:00:04,085 --> 00:00:06,651
mirror concentrates solar energy.

3
00:00:07,166 --> 00:00:10,724
Sunlight is already falling on this cooking vessel. But

4
00:00:10,724 --> 00:00:12,966
the light is spread over a large area, so

5
00:00:12,966 --> 00:00:16,286
the vessel heats up only slowly. Now we bring

6
00:00:16,286 --> 00:00:19,983
in a concave mirror. What can it change? The

7
00:00:19,983 --> 00:00:22,838
mirror collects light from a wide area, and redirects

8
00:00:22,838 --> 00:00:24,277
it towards one small region.

9
00:00:24,906 --> 00:00:26,959
Why do we draw the Sun's rays parallel? Take

10
00:00:26,959 --> 00:00:30,493
a lamp close to the mirror. Its rays spread

11
00:00:30,493 --> 00:00:33,036
out at a large angle. Move the lamp farther

12
00:00:33,036 --> 00:00:36,406
away, and the angle between the rays shrinks. The

13
00:00:36,406 --> 00:00:39,511
Sun is about one hundred and fifty million kilometres

14
00:00:39,511 --> 00:00:43,140
away. Across the width of a mirror, its rays

15
00:00:43,140 --> 00:00:46,652
are almost exactly parallel. So the mirror receives a

16
00:00:46,652 --> 00:00:48,517
bundle of nearly parallel sun rays.

17
00:00:49,079 --> 00:00:50,865
What does a concave mirror do to parallel rays?

18
00:00:50,865 --> 00:00:54,159
Three rays travel parallel to the principal axis. Each

19
00:00:54,159 --> 00:00:58,893
strikes the mirror at a different point, and reflects

20
00:00:58,893 --> 00:01:03,892
by the law of reflection. The dashed line is

21
00:01:03,892 --> 00:01:06,519
the normal. The reflected rays turn inwards, and all

22
00:01:06,519 --> 00:01:11,339
cross at one point. This point is the principal

23
00:01:11,339 --> 00:01:13,899
focus, F. Remember: a ray parallel to the axis

24
00:01:13,899 --> 00:01:14,750
reflects through F.

25
00:01:15,313 --> 00:01:18,057
How can we actually see the focus? Hold a

26
00:01:18,057 --> 00:01:20,253
small paper screen in front of the mirror. Close

27
00:01:20,253 --> 00:01:23,019
to the mirror, the bright patch is large and

28
00:01:23,019 --> 00:01:26,053
blurry. Move the screen slowly, and the patch becomes

29
00:01:26,053 --> 00:01:29,593
smaller and brighter. At one position it is the

30
00:01:29,593 --> 00:01:31,828
smallest and the brightest. This spot is the image

31
00:01:31,828 --> 00:01:36,933
of the Sun, at the focus. The distance from

32
00:01:36,933 --> 00:01:39,933
the pole to the focus is the focal length,

33
00:01:39,933 --> 00:01:43,540
f. Focused sunlight can burn paper, so this is

34
00:01:43,540 --> 00:01:47,184
shown as an animation. Never try it without a teacher.

35
00:01:47,686 --> 00:01:50,726
Why does the focal region get hot? Each sunlight

36
00:01:50,726 --> 00:01:52,679
ray carries some energy, shown here as a small

37
00:01:52,679 --> 00:01:56,866
marker. After reflection, the rays converge, and all the

38
00:01:56,866 --> 00:02:00,026
markers arrive in a much smaller region. Before the

39
00:02:00,026 --> 00:02:01,721
mirror, the light is spread over a wide area.

40
00:02:01,721 --> 00:02:05,496
At the focus, many reflected rays arrive in a

41
00:02:05,496 --> 00:02:08,514
small region. More of the collected sunlight reaches a

42
00:02:08,514 --> 00:02:12,066
smaller region, so the heating is stronger. The mirror

43
00:02:12,066 --> 00:02:14,862
does not create energy. It only redirects the sunlight

44
00:02:14,862 --> 00:02:16,177
that reaches it.

45
00:02:16,766 --> 00:02:18,673
Now let us turn the ray rule into a

46
00:02:18,673 --> 00:02:23,278
solar cooker. A large concave dish receives parallel sunlight.

47
00:02:23,278 --> 00:02:28,306
Every reflected ray converges near the focus, F. The

48
00:02:28,306 --> 00:02:30,756
cooking vessel is slid along the axis towards F.

49
00:02:30,756 --> 00:02:33,988
When the vessel is at F, it receives all

50
00:02:33,988 --> 00:02:37,966
the concentrated light. The vessel heats up strongly, enough

51
00:02:37,966 --> 00:02:39,537
to cook rice.

52
00:02:40,153 --> 00:02:42,023
Why must the cooker face the Sun? When the

53
00:02:42,023 --> 00:02:44,236
dish points straight at the Sun, the rays are

54
00:02:44,236 --> 00:02:46,573
parallel to its axis, and they meet at F,

55
00:02:46,573 --> 00:02:50,429
on the vessel. Turn the dish away, and the

56
00:02:50,429 --> 00:02:53,093
rays are no longer parallel to its axis. The

57
00:02:53,093 --> 00:02:56,003
bright region moves away from the vessel. The vessel

58
00:02:56,003 --> 00:03:00,581
misses the concentrated light, and its temperature falls. Turn

59
00:03:00,581 --> 00:03:03,873
the dish back towards the Sun, and the focus

60
00:03:03,873 --> 00:03:08,464
returns to the vessel. Always point the reflector towards the Sun.

61
00:03:09,066 --> 00:03:12,350
Can the vessel be placed anywhere? Before F, the

62
00:03:12,350 --> 00:03:14,486
rays are still converging, and they spread over a

63
00:03:14,486 --> 00:03:17,726
larger part of the vessel. At F, the ray

64
00:03:17,726 --> 00:03:21,866
bundle is the tightest, so the vessel receives the

65
00:03:21,866 --> 00:03:25,226
most concentrated light. Beyond F, the rays have crossed

66
00:03:25,226 --> 00:03:29,946
and are spreading again. Before F, spread. At F,

67
00:03:29,946 --> 00:03:33,946
most concentrated. After F, spread again. This is why

68
00:03:33,946 --> 00:03:36,717
the instruction says: place the vessel at the focus.

69
00:03:37,179 --> 00:03:41,419
Your turn. A: parallel rays strike a concave mirror.

70
00:03:41,419 --> 00:03:45,022
Where do the reflected rays go? B: the vessel

71
00:03:45,022 --> 00:03:47,999
can be placed before F, at F, or beyond

72
00:03:47,999 --> 00:03:50,676
F. Where should it go? C: which mirror is

73
00:03:50,676 --> 00:03:55,309
used: plane, convex or concave? D: what happens if

74
00:03:55,309 --> 00:03:57,879
the vessel is far beyond F? Take your time,

75
00:03:57,879 --> 00:03:59,950
and pause the video if you need to.

76
00:04:11,273 --> 00:04:14,350
Let us check. A: the reflected rays all pass

77
00:04:14,350 --> 00:04:19,273
through the focus, F. B: the vessel goes at

78
00:04:19,273 --> 00:04:22,451
F. C: a concave mirror, because only it makes

79
00:04:22,451 --> 00:04:25,866
parallel rays converge. D: the light is more spread

80
00:04:25,866 --> 00:04:28,824
out there, so move the vessel back towards F.

81
00:04:29,393 --> 00:04:31,113
Here is the whole story. The Sun is far

82
00:04:31,113 --> 00:04:33,733
away, so its rays arrive nearly parallel. They strike

83
00:04:33,733 --> 00:04:39,983
the concave reflector. The reflected rays converge at the

84
00:04:39,983 --> 00:04:44,493
focus, F. The vessel is placed at F. Concentrated

85
00:04:44,493 --> 00:04:49,193
sunlight heats it strongly. Parallel sun rays, concave reflector,

86
00:04:49,193 --> 00:04:54,309
focus, vessel, heating. Collect, reflect, focus, heat. That is

87
00:04:54,309 --> 00:04:57,533
the optical idea behind the solar cooker. Thank you

88
00:04:57,533 --> 00:04:59,084
